uth"}, {"id": "48827451-8085-43bd-a3f7-81e4e414edf4", "type": "concept", "title": "contingent truth"}, {"id": "c3962ae7-c19e-482c-bf4f-e4c825265380", "type": "concept", "title": "truth of reason"}, {"id": "229b23ac-3a33-43e7-8d48-61a6b492dc48", "type": "concept", "title": "truth of fact"}, {"id": "03dd7c5e-ab65-4886-a32f-88c34b38f8a8", "type": "concept", "title": "infinite analysis"}, {"id": "4f44de51-d177-46d6-8b9a-f6577c9a6605", "type": "concept", "title": "compossibility"}], "description": "This section covers the logical and metaphysical principles that structure Leibniz's system."}, {"id": "08014c1c-6f91-453b-9d8b-d5df91b20d81", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Harmony and theodicy", "children": [{"id": "cd66ab30-f930-40a0-ba45-706e2e313780", "type": "concept", "title": "pre-established harmony"}, {"id": "ed9bac67-edac-40d5-b8ff-c9e440081bb8", "type": "concept", "title": "best possible world"}, {"id": "2ace7923-bf58-403d-b90d-6d9d6f93f938", "type": "concept", "title": "possible world"}, {"id": "a03f99c8-3296-4f5d-bbc7-abb53c587069", "type": "concept", "title": "theodicy"}, {"id": "6043d29f-b8cf-4b8a-a28a-f6f5f14f703c", "type": "concept", "title": "evil"}, {"id": "1e1a2b01-92df-4587-89b2-2529329110b9", "type": "concept", "title": "optimism"}, {"id": "62354f44-72c5-4202-abb7-67bbed899799", "type": "concept", "title": "freedom"}, {"id": "9a5c5859-1040-4914-b63c-feb4addf4a04", "type": "concept", "title": "divine choice"}, {"id": "ff3ffc00-7a9b-4a4e-9945-5f1b3cc2739f", "type": "concept", "title": "miracle"}, {"id": "0e03c32e-4b09-4915-a116-3c3192f69c5f", "type": "concept", "title": "mind-body relation"}], "description": "Students evaluate Leibniz's attempt to reconcile mechanism, freedom, and divine goodness."}]}, {"id": "e28af132-8661-4e05-96ad-c88bae124dc0", "type": "topic", "title": "Locke: Ideas, Experience, and the Limits of Knowledge", "description": "Examine Locke's empiricism, critique of innate ideas, theory of ideas, and modest account of knowledge. Explain Locke's empiricist method and distinguish simple ideas, complex ideas, primary qualities, and secondary qualities.", "children": [{"id": "6c53efd7-21b8-4403-a0ea-4ba8a98ba59d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critique of innate ideas", "children": [{"id": "3368f5c4-72ef-4e20-b1cc-7ca72d6677d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Essay Concerning Human Understanding"}, {"id": "5218ffe2-8a66-44f8-9bac-c88dcf5a84ed", "type": "concept", "title": "innate idea"}, {"id": "57e4c56f-f9f6-4b9e-95c2-bb2f3297bf42", "type": "concept", "title": "tabula rasa"}, {"id": "c89f4ae7-60d9-4c32-857f-758897994da2", "type": "concept", "title": "sensation"}, {"id": "f7149545-3330-4bd7-8ca5-c57e7babd1dd", "type": "concept", "title": "reflection"}, {"id": "d8ea8ce2-156c-4424-8316-e045cc1d53ec", "type": "concept", "title": "experience"}, {"id": "facac416-1a12-46dd-8373-dbe6d11c9677", "type": "concept", "title": "simple idea"}, {"id": "45786324-6339-4622-a3c2-606636ee935f", "type": "concept", "title": "complex idea"}, {"id": "543b5605-2a3b-4a27-826b-8a686df75ff9", "type": "concept", "title": "association"}, {"id": "bf13d73e-1d93-4f14-9bee-48c62ea8c3cd", "type": "concept", "title": "empiricism"}], "description": "Students learn why Locke treats the mind as furnished by experience rather than built-in principles."}, {"id": "f34f2008-6c5e-44c9-a9c9-e10d2565bb05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Qualities and representation", "children": [{"id": "a5784267-a760-4324-a20b-07d190abe4af", "type": "concept", "title": "primary quality"}, {"id": "e2d61ef6-a7ce-4ee1-b159-4d8b2d3de019", "type": "concept", "title": "secondary quality"}, {"id": "8b0c85e6-6d5e-4830-a8a4-0a8c493ede28", "type": "concept", "title": "solidity"}, {"id": "f1d61ec8-f522-4897-a3e4-5856268c0d04", "type": "concept", "title": "extension"}, {"id": "8b2e3ff5-b504-43c2-90f8-a00021a9cac5", "type": "concept", "title": "motion"}, {"id": "2131a9c9-4c7a-4761-8cd1-be0291794f7e", "type": "concept", "title": "color"}, {"id": "3e0cdb85-ef1c-4346-8241-e11098acf460", "type": "concept", "title": "taste"}, {"id": "011d6e6c-4766-461b-aaf7-819bf40453ca", "type": "concept", "title": "representational realism"}, {"id": "55413593-f473-440b-bd39-a2524fbc2ad5", "type": "concept", "title": "idea"}, {"id": "db411714-e071-43af-852d-1f2d6507ed0f", "type": "concept", "title": "substratum"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces Locke's influential distinction between qualities in objects and ideas in minds."}, {"id": "81a4cb2b-7d67-497d-b2b3-e0de4795df44", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Knowledge, probability, and limits", "children": [{"id": "50d8c9ad-e553-4bda-907d-9d3db6596422", "type": "concept", "title": "intuitive knowledge"}, {"id": "f24542f2-143f-4e46-aa9f-8c02c05b22f7", "type": "concept", "title": "demonstrative knowledge"}, {"id": "7ab81184-cf9b-4cdf-baff-ac4e18439006", "type": "concept", "title": "sensitive knowledge"}, {"id": "84b17158-9dc2-45d6-a006-f44c4176049e", "type": "concept", "title": "probability"}, {"id": "cde3db2c-d986-4863-a719-7713d67fca5e", "type": "concept", "title": "agreement of ideas"}, {"id": "7648839e-fe9b-4d2b-9ab9-8d1bfe89696e", "type": "concept", "title": "nominal essence"}, {"id": "523fe9f3-bfa0-4731-9bd8-d24c9e9fb3d5", "type": "concept", "title": "real essence"}, {"id": "c510a718-81fb-4622-aabd-10c4888be79b", "type": "concept", "title": "substance"}, {"id": "b019852c-ae35-4522-a784-86aa41161ae1", "type": "concept", "title": "limits of understanding"}, {"id": "6a380c2c-ab87-4b20-8f8a-a7c296034080", "type": "concept", "title": "toleration of uncertainty"}], "description": "Students see Locke's epistemology as a discipline of intellectual humility."}]}, {"id": "d9c44b67-7c53-442e-934f-d1dd31c7617c", "type": "topic", "title": "Locke: Personal Identity, Property, and Political Authority", "description": "Connect Locke's theory of persons and responsibility to his political thought and Enlightenment liberalism. Analyze Locke's memory account of personal identity and his arguments about property, consent, and legitimate government.", "children": [{"id": "5dbeb3e2-2491-4fcc-8a20-9b3a817ef239", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Personal identity and consciousness", "children": [{"id": "85fb4524-dd4d-43db-9041-a4f21783ef90", "type": "concept", "title": "person"}, {"id": "a742edda-19e5-4902-b080-934ffedc48f8", "type": "concept", "title": "consciousness"}, {"id": "c37afae8-90d9-410d-aeaa-e38ca005d494", "type": "concept", "title": "memory"}, {"id": "3c32bfc9-a7e1-4684-a396-adaad601812f", "type": "concept", "title": "forensic term"}, {"id": "033e4839-2eaa-4014-a96e-e138a702ad61", "type": "concept", "title": "same man"}, {"id": "0a98fe89-c2dd-4949-a2dc-ffbc4551aee4", "type": "concept", "title": "same soul"}, {"id": "ca2180d0-8a05-4971-9224-751ccf7058e3", "type": "concept", "title": "same person"}, {"id": "156e64a1-28b0-47cb-a227-04dabdc6aa97", "type": "concept", "title": "responsibility"}, {"id": "495456c6-21ec-4dbf-b8ac-3f515389902f", "type": "concept", "title": "appropriation"}, {"id": "f0bbd572-1a53-444e-abfd-59841e2e8369", "type": "concept", "title": "Prince and Cobbler"}], "description": "Students learn Locke's shift from substance to consciousness in defining persons."}, {"id": "24bd3b17-4554-4058-962e-b11ece3c2473", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Property and labor", "children": [{"id": "ca6e33e1-ff92-48c9-99da-c2d96055a8e9", "type": "concept", "title": "state of nature"}, {"id": "35b93746-44f6-40aa-9e8e-40a78ba572b8", "type": "concept", "title": "natural law"}, {"id": "f64c2360-e008-4d24-b897-70527f8a2e84", "type": "concept", "title": "labor mixing"}, {"id": "b79645ed-0dd0-4c04-8cd6-c609e6b446df", "type": "concept", "title": "property right"}, {"id": "647ecec2-b8dd-4202-a7cd-d9f0a020caa3", "type": "concept", "title": "enough and as good"}, {"id": "66a85116-9c20-41e7-92b8-e9193fb58a37", "type": "concept", "title": "spoilage proviso"}, {"id": "bb9528bf-34b3-4f84-9544-736a86f4b86e", "type": "concept", "title": "money"}, {"id": "6104edb1-2822-4226-835a-d6ec7b076c05", "type"